<p class="cl-lead">Typing "cured in place pipe near me" into a search box gets you a map full of plumbers, most of whom do not own a liner. Lining is a specialty, and finding the company that actually does it, does it well and works your area is most of the battle. This page is how to do that.</p>

<h2>Search by state, not by town</h2>
<p>A lining crew's day is a truck, a boiler or UV unit, a wet-out setup and three or four trained people. That is an expensive mobilization, so installers cover large territories: a whole metro area at minimum, and often an entire state. That is why this site is organized by state. Use the <strong>Find Contractors</strong> button in the header, pick your state, and you will see the verified installer or installers who cover it, or go to the <a href="/all-contractors">full list of verified contractors</a> and browse by city. Every city page lists the installer who serves it along with completed projects nearby.</p>

<h2>What "verified" means here</h2>
<p>Each installer listed on CuredInPlacePipe.net has been checked for a current contractor's license, a documented history of lining work, and training on the liner and resin system they install. Prequalification requires at least five years in business and a strong public review record, followed by a phone interview before a listing goes live. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications">training and certifications</a> page explains why each of those matters and how to check them yourself for any contractor, listed here or not.</p>

<h2>What to ask on the first call</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>"Do your own crews do the lining, or do you sub it out?"</strong> Either answer can be fine, but you should know who will be in your yard.</li>
  <li><strong>"Which liner and resin system do you install, and are you trained on it?"</strong> A confident installer names the manufacturer without hesitating.</li>
  <li><strong>"Will I get the before-and-after camera recordings?"</strong> The answer should be yes, without being asked twice.</li>
  <li><strong>"Does the price include cleaning, reinstating every connection and a cleanout if I do not have one?"</strong> These are the items that turn into change orders.</li>
  <li><strong>"What is the warranty, and who stands behind it?"</strong> Manufacturer material coverage and installer workmanship coverage are separate.</li>
  <li><strong>"Have you lined a pipe like mine?"</strong> Cast iron under a slab, a clay lateral with a size change, an Orangeburg line that has started to deform: each is a little different. Many installers here post <a href="/all-contractors">completed projects</a> so you can see for yourself.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What happens next</h2>
<p>The installer schedules a camera inspection, often the same week. The recording tells them the length, diameter, material, number of connections, and whether any section needs to be excavated first rather than lined; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/repair">cured in place pipe repair</a> page explains that call. You get a written price. If you already have a dig-and-replace quote from someone else, bring it; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/cost">cured in place pipe cost</a> page shows what that comparison has looked like on real jobs. Then it is usually a one-day <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">installation</a>.</p>

